WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.042 --> 00:00:05.961
I am a late bloomer.

00:00:07.212 --> 00:00:09.714
In fact, a friend of mine
you may have heard of --

00:00:09.714 --> 00:00:11.383
Chris Rock --

00:00:11.383 --> 00:00:15.554
he once called me the most
late-blooming mofo he’d ever met.

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:16.721
(Laughter)

00:00:17.305 --> 00:00:20.058
Now, some people
might consider that snide,

00:00:20.058 --> 00:00:21.059
but I revel in it.

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:24.312
I’m 55,

00:00:24.312 --> 00:00:28.441
and I’m here in this curvy body
as someone who has done the work,

00:00:28.441 --> 00:00:29.734
lived the life

00:00:29.734 --> 00:00:32.696
walked the walk
in these very high heels --

00:00:32.696 --> 00:00:33.697
(Laughter)

00:00:33.697 --> 00:00:36.908
and therefore is qualified
to testify in the church

00:00:36.908 --> 00:00:38.368
and in the court of law

00:00:38.368 --> 00:00:41.871
that it does, in fact, get greater later.

00:00:42.414 --> 00:00:47.419
(Applause and cheers)

00:00:48.461 --> 00:00:50.755
Now coming to this
realization wasn’t easy.

00:00:50.755 --> 00:00:55.885
At the age of 38, I was a very successful
fashion advertising executive,

00:00:55.885 --> 00:00:59.222
and I was really living
what most people considered a dream life.

00:00:59.681 --> 00:01:01.850
I was jet-setting to fashion shows;

00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:03.893
I was receiving free designer clothes;

00:01:03.893 --> 00:01:06.313
I was double-kissing
my way across the globe.

00:01:06.479 --> 00:01:07.480
(Laughter)

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:08.481
I was.

00:01:09.024 --> 00:01:12.068
And, you know, it was everything
that I ever wanted it to be,

00:01:12.068 --> 00:01:16.239
and then one day I realized
I was only pretending to be happy.

00:01:17.657 --> 00:01:21.745
But I couldn’t blow up my good life
in my prime earning years, right?

00:01:22.954 --> 00:01:23.955
Wrong.

00:01:24.623 --> 00:01:27.626
Which leads me to lessons
my mother Lolly taught me.

00:01:27.959 --> 00:01:30.128
Lolly’s number one lesson:

00:01:30.128 --> 00:01:31.796
don’t settle.

00:01:33.214 --> 00:01:34.215
Don’t settle.

00:01:34.549 --> 00:01:37.010
Now I’m aware that my
well-paying, glamorous career

00:01:37.010 --> 00:01:40.472
is not exactly the humdrum,
“I hate my job” stereotype

00:01:40.472 --> 00:01:42.932
that most people equate with settling.

00:01:43.266 --> 00:01:45.226
But it was a settle for me,

00:01:45.226 --> 00:01:48.438
because when I actually did
quit my job at the age of 38,

00:01:48.438 --> 00:01:52.776
it was with the intention
that every day be a great adventure.

00:01:53.401 --> 00:01:55.487
Now sometimes it was
a very scary adventure,

00:01:55.487 --> 00:01:58.740
like being broke from the age of 40 to 45.

00:01:59.282 --> 00:02:02.619
But even still, I wouldn’t trade that
for the safe and settled version,

00:02:02.619 --> 00:02:05.747
because if I had, I would not
be here with y’all today.

00:02:06.706 --> 00:02:07.707
(Applause and cheers)

00:02:08.416 --> 00:02:09.417
Yeah.

00:02:09.793 --> 00:02:12.629
So you know how when you like,
buck the system

00:02:12.629 --> 00:02:14.923
and go against the status quo,

00:02:14.923 --> 00:02:17.258
it makes people really uncomfortable?

00:02:18.259 --> 00:02:20.512
And invariably, people will ask,

00:02:20.512 --> 00:02:22.222
“Where do you get your confidence?”

00:02:23.848 --> 00:02:24.849
(Laughter)

00:02:25.934 --> 00:02:29.020
Now some people mean it as a compliment,

00:02:29.020 --> 00:02:32.565
but very often it’s shady ...

00:02:32.565 --> 00:02:33.566
(Laughter)

00:02:33.566 --> 00:02:35.443
and it's a silent judgment.

00:02:36.277 --> 00:02:37.278
And to those people,

00:02:37.278 --> 00:02:40.532
I respond with a quote from this Brooklyn
poet you may have heard of,

00:02:40.532 --> 00:02:41.533
Jay-Z.

00:02:41.533 --> 00:02:42.534
(Laughter)

00:02:43.034 --> 00:02:44.661
“She get it from her mama.”

00:02:45.578 --> 00:02:46.579
I am she,

00:02:46.579 --> 00:02:50.625
and my mama is the epitome
of a grown-ass woman:

00:02:50.625 --> 00:02:53.753
someone who has always
been very comfortable in her skin.

00:02:54.587 --> 00:02:57.549
In 1965, my mom was 37 years old.

00:02:57.549 --> 00:02:58.800
She already had one child,

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:00.510
my big brother, Gerry,

00:03:00.510 --> 00:03:03.638
and she married my dad,
but she kept her maiden name.

00:03:03.972 --> 00:03:07.100
And then she had my sister
Stephanie and I back-to-back,

00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:08.309
but she continued to work

00:03:08.309 --> 00:03:11.980
because she refused to be
beholden to my dad for money.

00:03:12.647 --> 00:03:16.818
And I bet my mom was the only woman
in our neighborhood

00:03:16.818 --> 00:03:18.069
who cooked once a week.

00:03:18.445 --> 00:03:19.821
She made Sunday dinner.

00:03:19.821 --> 00:03:21.656
It was an extravaganza,

00:03:21.656 --> 00:03:22.782
but that’s all she did.

00:03:22.782 --> 00:03:24.033
She cooked one day a week.

00:03:24.868 --> 00:03:26.953
My mom is just amazing.

00:03:27.328 --> 00:03:32.834
And she also had this ability
of talking to her children about real life

00:03:32.834 --> 00:03:36.796
and making sure that we understood
the virtues of going your own way,

00:03:36.796 --> 00:03:40.467
which is why I believe
today at the age of 94,

00:03:40.467 --> 00:03:41.885
and a recent widow,

00:03:41.885 --> 00:03:44.345
my mom is still carving out ways

00:03:44.345 --> 00:03:48.850
to find and determine and define
her own version of happiness.

00:03:50.101 --> 00:03:51.603
She cooks for herself.

00:03:52.145 --> 00:03:54.731
She maintains her home
exactly as she sees fit.

00:03:55.607 --> 00:03:58.860
She enjoys champagne and R-rated films.

00:03:59.027 --> 00:04:00.028
(Laughter)

00:04:00.236 --> 00:04:01.237
(Applause)

00:04:02.655 --> 00:04:05.575
My mom has managed
to maintain her glamour,

00:04:05.575 --> 00:04:07.243
her sex appeal,

00:04:07.243 --> 00:04:09.621
you know, her independence.

00:04:10.121 --> 00:04:12.290
And I really hope
some of that rubs off on me.

00:04:13.249 --> 00:04:17.504
You know, recently I’ve been
thinking about one of the best lessons

00:04:17.504 --> 00:04:19.255
that my mom ever taught me,

00:04:19.255 --> 00:04:21.633
which is the literal beauty in aging.

00:04:22.383 --> 00:04:24.636
Now, we all know
that Black don’t crack, right?

00:04:26.137 --> 00:04:27.138
OK...

00:04:27.639 --> 00:04:28.640
Black don’t crack.

00:04:29.057 --> 00:04:33.520
So at the age of 50, my mom could have
easily passed for the age of 35.

00:04:34.437 --> 00:04:37.106
And you know, that’s back
during the time when people --

00:04:37.106 --> 00:04:39.067
women were really coy about their age.

00:04:39.067 --> 00:04:41.486
“Oh, a lady never tells her age.”

00:04:42.111 --> 00:04:43.863
My mom never subscribed to that.

00:04:44.113 --> 00:04:45.698
She was always proud of her age.

00:04:45.949 --> 00:04:47.951
As a matter of fact,

00:04:47.951 --> 00:04:49.911
she believes you may not tell your age,

00:04:49.911 --> 00:04:52.413
but your hands and your neck will.

00:04:52.705 --> 00:04:53.706
(Laughter)

00:04:53.998 --> 00:04:55.917
So make peace with aging,

00:04:55.917 --> 00:04:59.671
or prepare for an entire wardrobe
of gloves and turtlenecks.

00:04:59.712 --> 00:05:00.713
(Laughter)

00:05:04.467 --> 00:05:08.596
Yeah, my mom has always done
these wonderful things like that,

00:05:08.596 --> 00:05:10.515
but I wish she could rub off on everyone

00:05:10.515 --> 00:05:14.143
because I feel like now I’m looking
at even 20-somethings

00:05:14.143 --> 00:05:15.353
who have a fear of aging.

00:05:15.562 --> 00:05:17.647
I watch them on social media,

00:05:17.647 --> 00:05:22.193
like, you know, compulsively practiclng
the latest 10-second dance craze,

00:05:22.193 --> 00:05:23.653
and it feels like their angsty

00:05:23.653 --> 00:05:26.197
and asking, “Is that all there is?”

00:05:26.197 --> 00:05:27.490
And I just want to yell,

00:05:27.490 --> 00:05:30.910
“Yes, that is all there is
if all you’re going to do is settle

00:05:30.910 --> 00:05:33.454
for dancing to someone
else’s TikTok beat!”

00:05:33.830 --> 00:05:34.831
(Laughter)

00:05:34.831 --> 00:05:35.832
(Applause and cheers)

00:05:37.292 --> 00:05:39.961
Settling is very insidious.

00:05:40.128 --> 00:05:42.463
It keeps us dancing on this string,

00:05:42.463 --> 00:05:45.925
waiting for this elusive, better day
to miraculously appear.

00:05:46.718 --> 00:05:49.971
Now thanks to Lolly’s tutelage,
that’s not my story.

00:05:50.597 --> 00:05:53.016
In fact, I take each day as it comes

00:05:53.016 --> 00:05:55.018
but I try to make it better than the last.

00:05:55.018 --> 00:05:57.729
So, you know, I’m single ...

00:05:57.729 --> 00:05:59.814
but I’m always ready to mingle.

00:06:00.189 --> 00:06:01.190
(Laughter)

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:04.193
I’m an entrepreneur,

00:06:04.193 --> 00:06:06.821
but I keep multiple revenue streams.

00:06:07.530 --> 00:06:08.948
I’m a solo traveler,

00:06:08.948 --> 00:06:14.329
which means I’ve done the sepia version
of “Eat, Pray, Love” on six continents.

00:06:16.831 --> 00:06:18.875
Because I don’t settle.

00:06:19.042 --> 00:06:22.921
What that means is that I also
don’t second-guess my decisions,

00:06:22.921 --> 00:06:25.340
and I’m also not worried about my future

00:06:25.340 --> 00:06:27.342
because I’m firmly rooted in the present.

00:06:28.301 --> 00:06:30.428
Settling is a really sinister thing.

00:06:30.428 --> 00:06:33.056
It will keep you up at night
tossing and turning,

00:06:33.056 --> 00:06:34.766
trying to figure out why

00:06:34.766 --> 00:06:38.561
and trying to answer that age-old question
of “Is that all there is?”

00:06:38.978 --> 00:06:41.481
Personally, I don’t have time for that,

00:06:41.481 --> 00:06:45.151
because the only time I want to be
kept up all night long tossing and turning

00:06:45.151 --> 00:06:47.612
is when I’m in the company
of a fine-ass man.

00:06:47.737 --> 00:06:48.738
(Laughter)

00:06:48.821 --> 00:06:49.822
(Applause and cheers)

00:06:50.698 --> 00:06:51.699
That's it.

00:06:51.741 --> 00:06:52.742
(Applause)

00:06:54.118 --> 00:06:55.578
I wish I could tell you guys

00:06:55.578 --> 00:06:58.039
that I learned all these valuable
lessons from Lolly

00:06:58.039 --> 00:07:00.500
and they were instilled in me
and it was great,

00:07:00.500 --> 00:07:03.252
but alas, I am a late bloomer
in all regards.

00:07:03.544 --> 00:07:09.759
So I had to learn a couple of lessons
from the era of Bitchy Bevy.

00:07:12.011 --> 00:07:16.391
What kind of person
has 10 assistants in five years?

00:07:17.016 --> 00:07:18.643
Bitchy Bevy, that’s who.

00:07:18.643 --> 00:07:19.644
(Laughter)

00:07:20.186 --> 00:07:22.772
Now I didn’t start out my career
with a toxic attitude.

00:07:22.772 --> 00:07:25.733
No, initially I was really happy
to be in the fashion industry.

00:07:26.109 --> 00:07:29.487
You know, but then I began
to compare my trajectory to others,

00:07:29.487 --> 00:07:31.864
and I also began to feel burned out

00:07:31.864 --> 00:07:34.993
because I was burdened
by these personas that I had created

00:07:34.993 --> 00:07:37.787
that were allegedly going to help me
progress in my career.

00:07:37.870 --> 00:07:39.247
I made a couple of mistakes.

00:07:39.247 --> 00:07:42.166
One, I thought that being snarky
was a good career move.

00:07:42.792 --> 00:07:43.793
It wasn’t.

00:07:43.793 --> 00:07:45.962
I also thought I look good
in the color brown.

00:07:45.962 --> 00:07:46.963
I actually don’t.

00:07:46.963 --> 00:07:47.964
(Laughter)

00:07:47.964 --> 00:07:48.965
Yeah.

00:07:48.965 --> 00:07:49.966
(Laughter)

00:07:51.801 --> 00:07:53.720
And, you know, I just --

00:07:53.720 --> 00:07:55.888
in my dream montage,

00:07:55.888 --> 00:07:57.765
I wanted to get away from Bitchy Bevy.

00:07:57.765 --> 00:07:59.767
I wanted to get away from the color brown.

00:07:59.892 --> 00:08:02.645
And so in the movie version of my life,

00:08:02.645 --> 00:08:03.980
as soon as I quit my job,

00:08:03.980 --> 00:08:05.606
I’m a yoga guru.

00:08:07.191 --> 00:08:09.610
I’m extremely limber and very happy.

00:08:10.403 --> 00:08:11.988
Come to think of it though, guys,

00:08:11.988 --> 00:08:14.032
I’m actually limber and happy right now.

00:08:14.449 --> 00:08:15.575
But I would be lying --

00:08:15.575 --> 00:08:18.661
and I believe it is against
international law

00:08:18.661 --> 00:08:20.705
to lie during a TED Talk --

00:08:20.705 --> 00:08:21.706
(Laughter)

00:08:21.706 --> 00:08:23.166
so I’m not going to do that.

00:08:23.291 --> 00:08:28.337
And as a matter of fact, my insecurities
popped back up as late as last year.

00:08:28.421 --> 00:08:33.885
I was minding my business,
as one does, perusing social media,

00:08:33.885 --> 00:08:36.179
and I saw people excelling in a space

00:08:36.179 --> 00:08:38.973
where I, you know,
traditionally had a lot of success.

00:08:39.682 --> 00:08:41.309
So I’m looking at it and I’m like,

00:08:41.309 --> 00:08:43.811
“Well, why the hell
they ain’t call me for that job?”

00:08:44.896 --> 00:08:46.731
And I have this, like, angst,

00:08:46.731 --> 00:08:49.400
and then I realize
they didn’t call me for that job

00:08:49.400 --> 00:08:51.736
because you already said
you didn’t want that job.

00:08:51.736 --> 00:08:54.447
You told the universe
you weren’t into working like that.

00:08:54.447 --> 00:08:55.573
You don’t want a job --

00:08:55.573 --> 00:08:56.574
I really don’t.

00:08:56.699 --> 00:08:57.700
(Laughter)

00:08:57.784 --> 00:08:58.785
I’m not into it.

00:08:59.285 --> 00:09:00.995
So...

00:09:00.995 --> 00:09:02.497
that’s why it happened.

00:09:02.789 --> 00:09:07.710
And what I realize is that intellectually
I had grown and evolved,

00:09:07.710 --> 00:09:10.046
but emotionally ...

00:09:10.046 --> 00:09:11.923
I was Tom Petty

00:09:11.923 --> 00:09:14.092
and I was living in “Petticoat Junction.”

00:09:14.258 --> 00:09:15.259
(Laughter)

00:09:15.593 --> 00:09:18.596
I told y’all that brown
doesn’t look good on me;

00:09:18.596 --> 00:09:20.556
petty looks even worse.

00:09:20.890 --> 00:09:22.225
It’s not my shade.

00:09:23.392 --> 00:09:27.271
And so what I wound up
having to do was really get a grip.

00:09:28.397 --> 00:09:30.900
I had to assess a few things about myself,

00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:34.112
and I decided to do
a little self-help ritual called ...

00:09:34.112 --> 00:09:35.863
“Take a note, give a note.”

00:09:36.739 --> 00:09:37.740
It’s easy.

00:09:37.740 --> 00:09:40.910
When you see someone having something
that you believe you deserve,

00:09:40.910 --> 00:09:41.911
you take a note.

00:09:41.911 --> 00:09:43.496
You ask yourself a few questions.

00:09:43.579 --> 00:09:45.373
Is it something that you really want?

00:09:45.623 --> 00:09:48.417
Perhaps that person is better suited
than you are for that.

00:09:49.210 --> 00:09:50.294
Does the universe --

00:09:50.294 --> 00:09:52.547
is the universe conspiring
for you to have that?

00:09:52.880 --> 00:09:55.800
Really kind of try and be honest
with who you are

00:09:55.800 --> 00:09:57.176
and where you’re at in life.

00:09:57.885 --> 00:10:00.012
Once you do that, you take
a deep breath --

00:10:00.012 --> 00:10:01.222
(Inhales)

00:10:01.222 --> 00:10:04.809
and you say, “Their wins
have nothing to do with my worthiness.”

00:10:05.268 --> 00:10:07.645
And then you’re ready to give a note.

00:10:08.479 --> 00:10:10.481
You go on social media,

00:10:10.481 --> 00:10:12.024
and you say congratulations.

00:10:12.108 --> 00:10:13.568
Or my personal favorite,

00:10:13.568 --> 00:10:16.779
you pick up the phone,
like it’s the 20th century,

00:10:16.779 --> 00:10:18.281
and you say, “Congratulations,

00:10:18.281 --> 00:10:20.241
kudos, you did that, Al!

00:10:20.241 --> 00:10:21.242
You go, girl!”

00:10:21.242 --> 00:10:22.493
You do all the things.

00:10:22.785 --> 00:10:25.913
Instantly you feel
like a better human being

00:10:25.913 --> 00:10:29.083
because you have actually extended grace.

00:10:29.709 --> 00:10:31.210
You’ve extended grace.

00:10:31.210 --> 00:10:33.963
You’ve extended grace to someone else.

00:10:34.630 --> 00:10:37.800
And I believe that when you remove
malice from your heart,

00:10:37.800 --> 00:10:39.969
not only do you feel better,
you look better.

00:10:40.469 --> 00:10:44.056
I think you lose your frown lines
and your wrinkles lessen

00:10:44.056 --> 00:10:46.017
and your age spots disappear.

00:10:46.100 --> 00:10:48.853
I believe it’s better than Botox,

00:10:48.853 --> 00:10:49.854
extending grace.

00:10:49.854 --> 00:10:50.855
I do.

00:10:50.855 --> 00:10:51.856
(Applause)

00:10:53.149 --> 00:10:54.150
Yeah.

00:10:55.026 --> 00:10:57.695
No, but let me get back
to the note thing.

00:10:57.945 --> 00:11:02.700
So one of my favorite notes
is from Willie Shakespeare.

00:11:03.409 --> 00:11:05.411
“To thine own self be true.”

00:11:06.537 --> 00:11:08.456
Now we’ve all read self-help books,

00:11:08.456 --> 00:11:13.169
and the first line of defense
is always “Be your most authentic self.”

00:11:13.419 --> 00:11:14.545
And I believe in that.

00:11:15.129 --> 00:11:18.299
I believe that nobody can be you but you,

00:11:18.299 --> 00:11:21.052
so you might as well show up and show out.

00:11:21.719 --> 00:11:24.263
But here’s the quandary
that the bard never put forth.

00:11:24.639 --> 00:11:29.018
What if you don’t really know who you are
because you suppressed your inner self?

00:11:29.393 --> 00:11:31.062
You’ve suppressed the core of you.

00:11:31.062 --> 00:11:33.272
You’ve suppressed the best parts of you

00:11:33.272 --> 00:11:37.276
because you took on these other
identities and these personas

00:11:37.276 --> 00:11:39.904
in an effort to make your life better.

00:11:39.987 --> 00:11:42.156
Because, you know, we all
buy into some things

00:11:42.156 --> 00:11:45.326
about what we’re supposed to be doing
and who we’re supposed to be.

00:11:45.826 --> 00:11:47.453
So what if you squelch that?

00:11:47.495 --> 00:11:50.498
Because I know I had to excavate
to dig up a Little Brown Bevy.

00:11:51.040 --> 00:11:53.501
But the way I found her
was with three questions.

00:11:54.001 --> 00:11:55.253
Who am I at my core?

00:11:55.419 --> 00:11:56.712
How am I being perceived?

00:11:56.837 --> 00:11:58.422
How would I like to be perceived?

00:11:58.589 --> 00:11:59.590
Who am I at my core?

00:11:59.590 --> 00:12:02.635
At my core I’m looking
to authentically connect with people.

00:12:03.719 --> 00:12:06.681
I don't like a cursory,
you know, interaction,

00:12:06.681 --> 00:12:08.474
and I do not believe in networking.

00:12:08.724 --> 00:12:10.226
I like an authentic connection.

00:12:11.018 --> 00:12:13.479
I’m also curious and I’m adventurous

00:12:13.479 --> 00:12:14.772
and I’m kind

00:12:14.772 --> 00:12:16.148
and I’ve got big dreams.

00:12:16.732 --> 00:12:17.984
How am I being perceived?

00:12:18.192 --> 00:12:21.028
Well, y’all know
the nickname, Bitchy Bevy ...

00:12:21.028 --> 00:12:22.029
so, duh.

00:12:22.238 --> 00:12:23.406
But here’s the problem.

00:12:24.490 --> 00:12:27.201
There’s a lot of power in that persona,

00:12:27.201 --> 00:12:29.787
and I actually really enjoyed it
for a time, you know,

00:12:29.787 --> 00:12:32.123
because you can make
a lot of money being a bitch,

00:12:32.123 --> 00:12:33.249
especially in fashion.

00:12:33.457 --> 00:12:34.458
(Laughter)

00:12:35.209 --> 00:12:38.337
But it’s also incredibly
lonely and isolating,

00:12:38.337 --> 00:12:40.423
and I didn’t want to live
that life anymore.

00:12:40.423 --> 00:12:42.133
And so I decided to change my life.

00:12:42.383 --> 00:12:44.010
And I left all of that alone.

00:12:44.093 --> 00:12:46.971
I really did, like, just change my spirit.

00:12:47.305 --> 00:12:49.849
And leaving fashion obviously helped.

00:12:50.725 --> 00:12:52.518
And when I did that,

00:12:52.518 --> 00:12:56.022
all of a sudden, I let
Little Brown Bevy out to play.

00:12:57.398 --> 00:12:58.482
Little Brown Bevy.

00:13:00.151 --> 00:13:01.319
I love her so much.

00:13:01.777 --> 00:13:04.196
Little Brown Bevy is a nerdy girl,

00:13:04.196 --> 00:13:06.574
and so I let my nerdy pursuits
come out to play.

00:13:07.199 --> 00:13:10.870
I must have joined every museum
on Museum Mile in New York City.

00:13:11.078 --> 00:13:12.580
I began to travel the world

00:13:12.580 --> 00:13:15.082
just to look at architecture
I had always dreamed of.

00:13:15.416 --> 00:13:17.835
I learned how to be alone
without being lonely.

00:13:18.419 --> 00:13:19.670
My spirit shifted.

00:13:20.087 --> 00:13:21.756
I became a better person.

00:13:21.922 --> 00:13:23.090
You can ask people --

00:13:23.090 --> 00:13:24.300
I became a better person.

00:13:25.384 --> 00:13:28.679
And now I get to stand here
in front of you guys

00:13:28.679 --> 00:13:30.181
with no bravado,

00:13:30.181 --> 00:13:31.307
with nothing to prove,

00:13:31.307 --> 00:13:33.225
I tell you, with nothing to prove.

00:13:33.225 --> 00:13:35.186
I’m not trying to prove nothing to y’all.

00:13:35.186 --> 00:13:36.187
(Laughter)

00:13:36.437 --> 00:13:37.438
(Applause)

00:13:37.938 --> 00:13:39.690
(Cheers and applause)

00:13:39.690 --> 00:13:40.691
Thank you.

00:13:40.691 --> 00:13:41.692
(Applause and cheers)

00:13:42.943 --> 00:13:44.362
I have an open heart.

00:13:45.654 --> 00:13:46.947
And I can’t even believe

00:13:46.947 --> 00:13:51.410
that Little Brown Bevy
from 150th Street and Eighth Avenue,

00:13:51.410 --> 00:13:53.204
from the hamlet of Harlem,

00:13:53.204 --> 00:13:56.248
is now an award-winning radio and TV host,

00:13:56.248 --> 00:13:57.583
an author,

00:13:57.583 --> 00:13:58.834
an actress,

00:13:58.834 --> 00:14:00.086
a creative consultant.

00:14:00.336 --> 00:14:02.296
I would do all those things for free.

00:14:02.671 --> 00:14:03.881
But here’s the thing.

00:14:03.881 --> 00:14:06.133
I ain’t cheap,
and I’m definitely not free,

00:14:06.133 --> 00:14:07.968
so don't get any ideas.

00:14:07.968 --> 00:14:08.969
(Laughter)

00:14:08.969 --> 00:14:09.970
(Applause)

00:14:11.263 --> 00:14:15.142
But I am here in this
“Mama I made it” moment

00:14:15.142 --> 00:14:18.729
as someone who can show up
as her most Bevyest self

00:14:18.729 --> 00:14:20.106
because I’ve done the work.

00:14:21.023 --> 00:14:22.858
Yeah, my most Bevyest self.

00:14:23.275 --> 00:14:25.653
So, you know, I’m going to show up --

00:14:25.653 --> 00:14:27.655
some of you’ve met me --

00:14:27.655 --> 00:14:30.199
you know I’m vibrant
and boisterous, AKA loud.

00:14:30.783 --> 00:14:33.911
OK, you know that I’m going to show up,

00:14:33.911 --> 00:14:36.747
and I’ve got a pep in my high heel,
red-bottom step.

00:14:37.331 --> 00:14:39.458
I do have heaving cleavage.

00:14:39.708 --> 00:14:40.709
(Laughter)

00:14:40.709 --> 00:14:43.129
And I’ve got a tell-it-like-it-is
approach to life

00:14:43.129 --> 00:14:46.006
that’s always dosed with a ladle of love.

00:14:47.716 --> 00:14:49.927
It took me 55 years to get here.

00:14:50.428 --> 00:14:52.680
So, Chris Rock, you’re right.

00:14:53.222 --> 00:14:54.306
I’m a late bloomer.

00:14:54.682 --> 00:14:56.434
And that’s OK.

00:14:56.434 --> 00:14:58.144
Because I’m right on time,

00:14:58.144 --> 00:15:00.729
Because it gets greater later.

00:15:00.980 --> 00:15:01.981
Thank you.

00:15:01.981 --> 00:15:08.946
(Applause and cheers)

